WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.200
-Welcome back to the show.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.734
That's a hometown crowd
right here.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:05.400
-Oh, my God, this is...
-Yeah, I mean...

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:09.534
I want to know
about you guys growing up

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:11.601
because your
New York City brothers.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.567
You're all brothers from New
York City. You grew up here.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.734
And how long have you
been performing together?

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:18.667
I know you know
each other forever.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:19.801
-Yes, we do.

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:21.601
-We started off street
performing

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.400
in Washington Square Park
in Central Park,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.067
like 18 years ago.

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.767
And you were maybe seven?
-I was seven years old.

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:29.534
-Wow. Yeah.
Were you really?

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:30.667
-No, I actually was.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.000
-You were seven years old.
-Seven years old.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.834
-And how did you get into
busking? What would you do?

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.367
-So we just knew nobody
in the industry.

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:39.067
So we were like, "I guess this
is our in into the industry."

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.934
-Like, the funny thing was that
as we did it more,

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:43.601
we ended up making
a lot of money

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:45.467
and people --
when I say that to people,

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:46.901
they're like, "Wow,
you must have been really good."

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:49.234
But then I go, "Imagine
you're walking through the park

00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:50.734
and you see a 7-year-old boy

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:53.567
singing 'New York State of Mind'
by Billy Joel.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:54.801
You would feel like
a really bad person

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:56.300
if you didn't give him
all of your money."

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.701
-Yeah. And you would do this.
Who would take you there?

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.567
Just Dad or...?

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.400
-I -- We would --
I think our parents would go,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.300
"Here's --" How much
was a bus back then?

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.868
I don't know.
"Here's a quarter for the bus."

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:10.200
This was in 1942,
I don't know. A quarter.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.601
[ Laughter ]
-No. Probably $1.50, $2. Yeah.

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:14.067
-Take the bus.
We'll see you when you get back.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.234
Let us know how it goes.
That kind of thing.

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.267
-Really?
-Yeah. We wanted to do it. Yeah.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.234
-And you just
went and sang karaoke.

00:01:19.234 --> 00:01:20.701
Did you have microphones?
No, you didn't have...

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:22.767
-We had microphones. We did.
Right?

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.667
-We did have microphones
and we tap danced.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.300
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.634
-You tap danced?
-I don't see
anything funny about that.

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.434
No, no.
-That image? No.

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:32.367
-We actually have a clip
weirdly enough.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:35.567
Here's AJR busking in
New York City back in 2005.

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:36.934
Take a look at this.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:38.267
[ Shoes tapping ]

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:51.801
Wow!

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:53.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:55.834
That's impressive.
-Is it? Thank you.

00:01:55.834 --> 00:01:57.901
-That's impressive.

00:01:57.901 --> 00:02:00.901
What was on your T-shirts there?
It says TB3.

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:04.033
-Oh, it's so embarrassing.
Do you want to say it?

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:07.033
-The band name was Tapping
Brothers to the Third Power.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:08.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:10.534
[ Rimshot ]

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:12.601
-Why didn't you...

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:14.267
Why didn't you keep that name
now?

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.300
The Tapping...
-That would have sounded good

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.767
coming out of your mouth.
"Now welcome Tapping..."

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:20.667
-Tapping Brothers to the Third
Power, ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.634
That's how we know them.
Yes.

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:25.667
I want to talk -- Your album
just came out at midnight.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:27.000
How does it feel?
Congratulations,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.767
and thank you
for doing our show.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:29.834
-Thank you.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.000
-It means a lot to us
that you're here.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.601
The album title is called
"The Maybe Man."

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:36.634
What's the story
behind the title?

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:38.534
-We had a crazy couple
of years writing this album.

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.901
Some crazy family stuff
happened to us.

00:02:40.901 --> 00:02:43.033
Our dad actually got sick
and passed away

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:44.601
during the making of this album.

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:46.000
When that happens,
it's really sad.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.467
But it's also like, "Oh, my God,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:48.901
I now know what matters
in life."

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.200
It's like all these
other opportunities

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.501
and choices
that you were worried about,

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:54.367
it's like, nope,
this is what matters in life.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:57.634
It's friends, and it's
making art that you really love.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:02:59.234
And I think that's a lot
what it's about.

00:02:59.234 --> 00:03:03.033
It's like becoming 30 and being
in your 30s and realizing like,

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.734
oh, this is what I want
my life to be now.

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:06.501
-Gosh. Wow. That's tough.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:09.601
Tough couple of years
dealing with this.

00:03:09.601 --> 00:03:12.133
Well, he's got to be
proud of you guys.

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:14.267
-Oh, he was -- Yeah, he was
definitely our biggest fan.

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.634
I would say. He's the whole
reason we do this honestly.

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:18.934
He was such a music lover.
And he said "I want to give you

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.033
my knowledge of music
and go do something with it."

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:23.567
-At the end of every show,
I would always look forward to

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.133
looking out at him
in the audience,

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.734
because at the end of the show,

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:29.534
he would always stand up
and just go like this.

00:03:29.534 --> 00:03:31.200
Yeah.
-That's a good dad.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.400
That's a good supportive dad.
-There we go. Yeah.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.234
[ Applause ]

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.467
-Well, you announced that
you're going on an arena tour

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.133
with the album next year.
-Yes.

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:40.534
-And it included,

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:43.400
I couldn't help but notice,
a stop at Madison Square Garden.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.167
And I was going to say...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:49.133
...to kids who were born
and raised in New York,

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:51.567
how does it feel to be playing
Madison Square Garden?

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:53.801
-I don't think I'm going
to really process

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.367
until we get up there.
We grew up like

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.567
five blocks downtown, literally
from Madison Square Garden.

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.868
So I'm hoping we can say --
maybe we can be the only people

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:01.000
ever to,
like, leave our house

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.133
and walk
to Madison Square Garden

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:05.200
to go to actually go play there.
Maybe that'd be...

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.300
-Yeah, you might be one of the
first people to ever do that.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.434
-Yeah, but it's insane.
-And I got to say, I'm a fan of

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.167
"Yes, I'm a Mess."
-Thank you.

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:14.100
-Which you're performing
tonight?

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.300
-Yes, we are.
-On our show.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:23.801
The video, by the way,
turned out fantastic.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:27.434
-Thank you.
-This is -- You were --

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:28.901
You were dressed
as a Times Square Elmo.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.601
-I was, yes. That is me.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.601 --> 00:04:32.000
Was that always
the plan of the video?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.367
-No, that was not.

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:34.734
This is how it went.
There's a little message

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:36.534
in the beginning of the video
that says what happened.

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:38.434
What happened was we spent
a lot of money

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:41.300
on what was supposed
to be the music video.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.300
We ended up finishing it
and it just didn't feel right.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.167
Something in our gut was like,
"This is not going to work."

00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:47.267
And the video was needed
like the next day.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:48.734
And so we have to come up
with an idea

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:49.968
and I don't know
where it came from,

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:52.267
but I said me in an Elmo suit
in Times Square

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.567
trying to get tourists
to take photos with me.

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:55.701
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:57.300
-Was it hard for people to take?

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.167
-It was nearly impossible, yes.

00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:00.934
I think like maybe I got three

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:03.467
and I asked 500 people
to do this.

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:05.400
-It's a tough gig. I mean...

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.267
-It's -- So, what I did realize

00:05:07.267 --> 00:05:10.033
was that the people,
the other dress-up figures,

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:12.367
are so good at this,
they're amazing.

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:13.834
And the reason I -- The way
I found out

00:05:13.834 --> 00:05:17.334
how was I kind of started
to kind of assimilate with them.

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:20.067
And they -- So I went in, I was
like, "Hey, we're all Elmo."

00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:21.834
And they started
to really befriend me.

00:05:21.834 --> 00:05:24.033
They were like, "Oh, come on
in." They were gassing me up.

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:25.901
"That's a great Elmo costume.
Where'd you get it?"

00:05:25.901 --> 00:05:27.234
And then I was getting
really excited.

00:05:27.234 --> 00:05:29.934
And then two Minnie Mouse
costumes come over

00:05:29.934 --> 00:05:31.667
and they go,
"Let us be in the video."

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.267
And they start doing a kick line
with me.

00:05:33.267 --> 00:05:34.400
I'm like, "This is fun."

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.501
And then we stop
and they're like, "That was fun.

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:39.067
You know, it's really cold out.
And like, do you mind giving us,

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:40.734
you know, a little something?"
And I went, "Yeah."

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:43.601
And I kind of gave them all my
money because we're friends.

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:44.901
And then I walked away
and I was like,

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:46.834
"They're not my friends."
-No.

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:47.934
They're genius.

00:05:47.934 --> 00:05:50.400
They are absolutely genius
at what they do.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.467
Yeah. Here's a photo of you,
by the way.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.567
Getting arrested
in Times Square.

00:05:56.567 --> 00:06:00.234
Yeah. It's a very,
very fantastic video.

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:02.767
And you're going to perform it
for us later on tonight.

00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:05.567
And congratulations.
The album is officially out.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:10.534
"The Maybe Man," AJR.
It's available right now

00:06:10.534 --> 00:06:12.434
and they're performing
for us after the break.

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:13.801
I'm so happy
to talk to you guys

00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:15.067
and thank you again
for being here.

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:16.801
Come right back, everybody.
More "Tonight Show."

